Once a range request is answered with 200 and the entire resource,
Ladybird stops asking that server for ranges, whatever its Accept-Ranges
header says, and lets the fetch under way run on.

Gecko does the same in ChannelMediaResource::OnStartRequest(): A 200
answering a range request at a nonzero offset sets acceptsRanges to
false, so the resource isn’t seekable. Blink accepts such a 200 in
ResourceMultiBufferDataProvider::DidReceiveResponse(), and as the bytes
before the position it asked for arrive, discards them rather than
asking for the range again.
